Let $L_A(\infty)=U_A^-\cap L(\infty)$ be the integral residue lattice of [[def-integral-residue-lattices-of-the-negative-half-and-highest-weight-modules]], and let $(\ ,\ )_0$ be the $\mathbb Q$-valued symmetric bilinear form on the crystal limit $L(\infty)/qL(\infty)$ obtained by reducing the normalized polarization at $q=0$ ([[lem-crystal-lattice-polarizations-are-perfect-at-q-zero]]); the crystal basis $\mathcal B(\infty)$ is orthonormal for $(\ ,\ )_0$ and $(\ ,\ )_0$ is a unit Gram form on every finite root component.

Then:
(i) the canonical map $L_A(\infty)/qL_A(\infty)\to L(\infty)/qL(\infty)$ is an isomorphism of $\mathbb Z$-modules; equivalently $L_A(\infty)/qL_A(\infty)$ is a free $\mathbb Z$-module with basis the image of $\mathcal B(\infty)$;
(ii) for $u\in L_A(\infty)/qL_A(\infty)$ and $b\in\mathcal B(\infty)$ the coefficient of $b$ in $u$ equals $(u,b)_0\in\mathbb Z$, and $\mathcal B(\infty)=\{u\in L_A(\infty)/qL_A(\infty):(u,u)_0=1\}$;
(iii) the integrality input: the normalized polarization is $A_0$-valued on $L(\infty)$ and takes values in the localization $A_{\mathrm{loc}}=A[[r]_i!^{-1}]$ on the divided-power form $U_A^-$ ([[thm-the-negative-half-bilinear-form-is-symmetric-nondegenerate-and-integral]]); the two normalizations differ by the explicit color-degree factor recorded in that theorem, whose factors are Laurent polynomials in $A$, and the intersection is $A_0\cap A_{\mathrm{loc}}=A_{\mathbb Z}$, the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\mathbb Q(q)$ generated by $q$ and the $(1-q^{2n})^{-1}$ $(n\ge1)$; consequently $(L_A(\infty),L_A(\infty))_0\subseteq A_{\mathbb Z}$ and, since $A_{\mathbb Z}/qA_{\mathbb Z}=\mathbb Z$, the reduced form is $\mathbb Z$-valued. These are Kashiwara's (6.1.7)–(6.1.11) and Proposition 6.1.1;
(iv) $\tilde f_iL_A(\infty)\subseteq L_A(\infty)$ for every $i$, and the induced map on residues preserves the image of $\mathcal B(\infty)$; the classes of the $\tilde f$-monomials of $\mathbf 1$ generate $L_A(\infty)/qL_A(\infty)$ over $\mathbb Z$.

No freeness of $U_A^-$ in any root degree, no $A$-valuedness of the divided-power form on $U_A^-$ and no statement about the highest weight modules are asserted here; the $V(\lambda)$ analogue follows from the globalization theorem.

For every $\beta\in Q_+$ choose a $\mathbb Q(q)$-basis $\{u_i\}$ of $U_q^+[\beta]$ and let $\{v_i\}$ be the dual basis of $U_q^-[-\beta]$ under the pairing of [[lem-the-drinfeld-hopf-pairing-is-nondegenerate-in-each-root-degree]]: $(u_i,v_j)=\delta_{ij}$. Put
$$\Theta_\beta:=\sum_i u_i\otimes v_i\in U_q^+[\beta]\otimes U_q^-[-\beta],\qquad \Theta_0:=1\otimes1 .$$
Then:
(i) $\Theta_\beta$ does not depend on the choice of the basis $\{u_i\}$;
(ii) for any two integrable chosen-weight modules $M,N$ with finite-dimensional weight components and bounded-above weight support (in particular for objects of the finite-weight-space highest weight category $\mathcal O_{\mathrm{int}}$ and for finite-degree polynomial type-A modules), and any $m\otimes n\in M\otimes N$, only finitely many $\beta$ satisfy $\Theta_\beta(m\otimes n)\ne0$; hence $\Theta=(\Theta_\beta)_\beta$ is an element of the completion of [[def-root-height-completion-for-weightwise-quasi-r-intertwiners]] and acts on $M\otimes N$;
(iii) the action is locally finite in the filtration: for every $N_0$ and every $m\otimes n$ only finitely many $\beta$ with $\operatorname{ht}(\beta)\ge N_0$ contribute, so every weight component of $M\otimes N$ is stable under the tail of $\Theta$.

Let $\Theta=(\Theta_\beta)$ be the intertwiner of [[thm-the-weightwise-quasi-r-intertwiner-exists-and-is-unique]], and let $\Gamma=(\Gamma_\beta)$ be the unique family in $\prod_\beta U_q^+[\beta]\otimes U_q^-[-\beta]$ with $\Gamma_0=1\otimes1$ satisfying the opposite intertwining recursion (the mirror of the displayed degree recursions with the roles of the two factors exchanged).

Then, in the completed tensor square and after evaluating weightwise:
(i) $\Gamma\Theta=1=\Theta\Gamma$;
(ii) the two threefold coproduct factorizations hold:
$$(\Delta\otimes\operatorname{id})(\Theta_\beta)=\sum_{0\le\gamma\le\beta}(\Theta_\gamma)_{13}\,(1\otimes K_\gamma\otimes1)\,(\Theta_{\beta-\gamma})_{23},$$
$$(\operatorname{id}\otimes\Delta)(\Theta_\beta)=\sum_{0\le\gamma\le\beta}(\Theta_\gamma)_{13}\,(1\otimes K_{-\gamma}\otimes1)\,(\Theta_{\beta-\gamma})_{12},$$
where the subscripts denote the tensor positions and $K_\gamma=\prod_iK_i^{n_i}$ for $\gamma=\sum_in_i\alpha_i$;
(iii) every equality in (i)–(ii) is an equality of weightwise-finite families: on each finite-dimensional weight component both sides have only finitely many nonzero terms, and no multiplication of uncontrolled formal infinite sums occurs;
(iv) uniqueness fails without the degree-zero normalization: the family with $\Theta'_0=\lambda\cdot1\otimes1$ satisfies the same recursions for every $\lambda\in\mathbb Q(q)^\times$, and the normalization $\Theta_0=1\otimes1$ is part of the definition.

Let $M_1,\dots,M_d$ be based integrable modules in the finite-weight-space highest weight category $\mathcal O_{\mathrm{int}}$ with crystal bases $(L_j,B_j)$ and lower global bases $\mathbf G_j$ ([[def-lower-global-basis-of-a-quantum-highest-weight-module]]), or finite-degree polynomial type-A modules used by the Kazhdan–Lusztig truncation route. Let $\Theta$ be the quasi-$R$ intertwiner of [[thm-the-weightwise-quasi-r-intertwiner-exists-and-is-unique]] and define the **tensor bar** on $M_1\otimes\cdots\otimes M_d$ by
$$\overline{m_1\otimes\cdots\otimes m_d}:=\Theta\bigl((\overline{m_1\otimes\cdots\otimes m_k})\otimes(\overline{m_{k+1}\otimes\cdots\otimes m_d})\bigr)$$
for any $1\le k<d$, the factors being defined inductively and the sum being the weightwise-finite action of [[lem-homogeneous-dual-basis-sums-are-basis-independent-and-weightwise-finite]].

Then:
(i) the tensor bar is a well-defined semilinear involution: it is $\mathbb Q$-linear in each factor, satisfies $\overline{a\cdot x}=\psi(a)\overline{x}$ for $a\in\mathbb Q(q)$, $\overline{\overline{x}}=x$, and is compatible with the $U_q$-action, $\overline{u\cdot x}=\psi(u)\cdot\overline{x}$;
(ii) it is independent of the choice of $k$ (parenthesization independence), and is consistent under all rebracketings, so it defines a bar on every finite tensor product;
(iii) it preserves the crystal lattices: $\overline{L_1\otimes\cdots\otimes L_d}\subseteq L_1\otimes\cdots\otimes L_d$ modulo the twist between the $q=0$ and $q=\infty$ charts, in the sense that the tensor product crystal basis remains a crystal basis ([[thm-crystal-basis-tensor-product-rule]]);
(iv) it preserves the divided-power integral tensor lattices $M_{1,A}\otimes\cdots\otimes M_{d,A}$ and the expansion of the bar on tensor products of lower global basis elements is dominance-triangular with diagonal $1$ ([[lem-quasi-r-divided-power-integrality-on-based-lattices]]), so the tensor product of lower based modules is again a based module with a normalized integral bar;
(v) no assertion is made for an arbitrary two-sided-unbounded integrable module, for a semi-infinite direct limit, or for the universal $R$-matrix: only finite tensor products of modules with bounded-above weight support are covered.

Let $\mathfrak g=\mathfrak{sl}_2$, $I=\{1\}$, $a_{11}=2$, $q_1=q$, and let $V(\omega)=L_1(1)$ be the two-dimensional simple module with basis $v$ (weight $\omega$) and $w:=F_1v$ (weight $\omega-\alpha_1$), so that $Ev=0$, $Fv=w$, $Ew=v$, $Fw=0$, $Kv=qv$, $Kw=q^{-1}w$ ([[thm-quantized-sl-two-string-formulas]]). Let $A=\mathbb Z[q,q^{-1}]$, $A_0=\mathbb Q[q]_{(q)}$ and $A_\infty=\psi(A_0)$.

Then:
(a) $V_A(\omega)=Av\oplus Aw$, $L(\omega)=A_0v\oplus A_0w$, $L(\omega)^-=A_\infty v\oplus A_\infty w$, and $E(\omega)=V_A(\omega)\cap L(\omega)\cap L(\omega)^-=Av\oplus Aw$;
(b) the globalization map $E(\omega)\to L(\omega)/qL(\omega)$ is an isomorphism, so $(V_A(\omega),L(\omega),L(\omega)^-)$ is a balanced triple; the lower global basis is $G(b_\omega)=v$, $G(b_{\omega-\alpha_1})=w$, where $b_\mu$ denotes the crystal element of weight $\mu$; both elements are bar-invariant;
(c) the string ideal $f_1V(\omega)=Aw$ and its integral lattice satisfy $(f_1V(\omega))_A\cap L(\omega)\cap L(\omega)^-=Aw$ with residue basis $b_{\omega-\alpha_1}$, an instance of the string-ideal balancedness;
(d) on $V\otimes V$ the tensor bar of [[def-balanced-triple-and-globalization-map]] applied with the rank-one quasi-$R$ twist is the involution
$$\overline{v\otimes v}=v\otimes v,\quad \overline{v\otimes w}=v\otimes w,\quad \overline{w\otimes v}=w\otimes v+(q-q^{-1})v\otimes v,\quad \overline{w\otimes w}=w\otimes w+(q-q^{-1})v\otimes w,$$
and it preserves the integral lattice $V_A\otimes_AV_A$ with coefficients in $A$;
(e) the 'wrong' lattice $L':=A_0(v+w)\oplus A_0(qv)\subseteq V(\omega)$ is free of rank $2$, generates $V(\omega)$ over $\mathbb Q(q)$ and contains the bar-invariant element $v+w\equiv v+w\bmod qL'$, but $L'$ is not balanced with respect to $V_A(\omega)$ and $A_\infty$: the map $V_A(\omega)\cap L'\cap A_\infty L'\to L'/qL'$ is not an isomorphism. Hence bar-invariance plus the congruence modulo $q$ do not by themselves manufacture a global basis.

Work in $V(\omega)=L_1(1)$ for $\mathfrak{sl}_2$ with basis $v,w$ as in [[ex-balanced-triple-for-the-two-dimensional-quantum-sl-two-module]], and consider the free $A_0$-lattice
$$L':=A_0(v+w)\oplus A_0(qv)\subseteq V(\omega).$$
Then:
(a) $L'$ is a free $A_0$-module of rank $2$ generating $V(\omega)$ over $\mathbb Q(q)$, and does not satisfy the crystal-lattice axioms of [[def-crystal-lattice-and-crystal-basis]]: $\tilde e_1(v+w)=v\notin L'$, and $L'$ is not weight-decomposed because $v+w\notin L'_{\omega}\oplus L'_{\omega-\alpha_1}$;
(b) the element $v+w\in L'$ is $\psi$-invariant and satisfies $v+w\equiv v+w\bmod qL'$, so it meets the two normalizations of [[def-lower-global-basis-of-a-quantum-highest-weight-module]] (bar invariance and the $q$-congruence) without being a global basis element;
(c) the balanced part $E'=V_A(\omega)\cap L'\cap A_\infty L'$ maps into $L'/qL'$ but not onto it: the image is spanned by the classes of $v+w$ and $qv$, while the class of $v$ is not in the image; hence $(V_A(\omega),L',A_\infty L')$ is not a balanced triple;
(d) consequently bar invariance plus the congruence modulo $q$ do not imply balancedness, do not produce a global-basis parametrization and do not force uniqueness: the hypothesis that $L$ be a crystal lattice (or the balanced-triple isomorphism) is indispensable. The contrast with the true triple $(V_A(\omega),L(\omega),A_\infty v\oplus A_\infty w)$ of [[ex-balanced-triple-for-the-two-dimensional-quantum-sl-two-module]] is the point of the example.
